WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.402
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.268 --> 00:00:09.310
SPEAKER: As you deploy
workloads in Google Cloud,

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:12.190
you need centralized visibility
into the security posture

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:13.990
and status of your environment.

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:15.790
You need a way to
surface vulnerabilities

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:17.620
and malicious activity
with clear ways

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.960
to take action against them.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.600
Security Command Center is a
built-in security management

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.250
tool for Google
Cloud Platform that

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:27.070
helps you prevent, detect,
and remediate vulnerabilities

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:29.400
and threats.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.310
You can identify misconfigured
virtual machines, containers,

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:35.460
networks, storage, and identity
and access management policies,

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:38.070
as well as web application
vulnerabilities,

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.500
and act on them before
they damage your business.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:41.880
With Security
Command Center, you

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.100
can perform several
security-related actions

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.090
from a single
centralized dashboard.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:50.700
Let's get started by
completing three steps.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:54.770
Step one, set up
Cloud IAM permissions.

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:56.210
To get started,
you need to ensure

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:58.970
you have the correct
permissions set up in Cloud IAM.

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:01.250
These roles can be enabled
from the GCP console

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:03.170
IAM and admin page.

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:04.879
To view the Security
Command Center,

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:07.640
you must have the Security
Center Admin Viewer role.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.070
To make changes, you must have
an appropriate editor role,

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.590
like Security
Center Admin Editor.

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.040
To set up Security
Command Center

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.900
for the first time
for your organization,

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:16.990
you'll need five roles--

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.820
Organization Admin,
Security Center Admin,

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:22.700
Security Center Settings
Admin, Security Admin,

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:24.260
and Service Account Creator.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:26.270
Step two, choose services.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:27.260
Head to the console.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.420
On the left-hand panel,
navigate to Security

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.970
and then click Security
Command Center.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:36.380
On the Organization dropdown
list, select your organization.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.995
Note-- if this is the first
time your organization is using

00:01:38.995 --> 00:01:40.370
Security Command
Center, you will

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:42.078
be directed through
a sign-up flow, where

00:01:42.078 --> 00:01:44.762
you will choose your services.

00:01:44.762 --> 00:01:46.220
For organizations
that have already

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:48.560
completed the sign-up flow,
you may review and refine

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:51.580
your services from the UI.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:54.730
To do so, click on Add Security
Sources to review and enable

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:56.260
the services that
Security Command

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.030
Center will use to
show you the security

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:01.480
state of your resources.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:04.630
Our built-in services are
Security Health Analytics,

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:07.630
which can identify misconfigured
virtual machines, containers,

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:11.740
networks, storage, and identity
and access management policies.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:13.660
The built-in capabilities
in the standard tier

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:16.090
include scans for high-severity
misconfigurations,

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:19.805
such as open storage buckets.

00:02:19.805 --> 00:02:22.180
When using Security Health
Analytics in the premium tier,

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:24.760
you get scans for broader
security misconfigurations,

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:27.550
such as API keys
needing to be rotated.

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:30.430
In addition, you get compliance
monitoring and security posture

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:36.850
management against PCI DSS, CIS
GCP 1.0, NIST 800-53, and ISO

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:40.870
27001 recommendations, among
other security best practices.

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:43.960
For premium users, there are
additional services available.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.540
Web Security Scanner
manage scan capabilities,

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:48.850
which automatically detect
new public-facing web

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:50.770
applications deployed
on Google Cloud

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.530
and can detect vulnerabilities
in those applications,

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:56.570
including those from
categories in the OWASP top 10,

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:59.590
such as cross-site scripting,
use of clear text passwords,

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:01.840
and outdated libraries.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.880
Event Threat Detection,
which uses log data

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:06.370
from inside your systems and
watches your organization's

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:09.150
Cloud Logging stream to detect
threats like brute-force SSH,

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:13.030
Cloud IAM abuse, malware,
and data exfiltration.

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:14.462
Container Threat
Detection, which

00:03:14.462 --> 00:03:16.420
can detect some of the
most common threats seen

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:19.000
in the containerized
workloads running on GKE,

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.730
such as adversaries dialing
home with reverse shells

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:23.740
or the execution of
binaries in libraries

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:26.810
that did not come from
your secure build pipeline.

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:28.630
You can also integrate
with other services

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:32.540
to have their findings surfaced
in Security Command Center.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:34.700
Cloud Armor, which is
a customizable service

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:37.970
for protecting applications
from DDoS or web attacks.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:39.650
Cloud Armor sends
any allowed traffic

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:42.890
spike and increasing deny ratio
findings to Security Command

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:43.760
Center.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.250
Data Loss Prevention, which
shows storage buckets that

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:48.530
contain sensitive
and regulated data,

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:50.210
integrates and
surfaces its findings

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:52.040
into Security Command Center.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:54.440
And partner solutions
from many partners,

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:55.940
such as Chef
Automate, which can be

00:03:55.940 --> 00:04:00.720
integrated into Security
Command Center and enabled here.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:02.640
You can enable or
disable a service

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.170
by clicking the toggle
next to the service name.

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:07.710
The services will be enabled
at the organization level.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.200
Optionally, you can enable
or disable your services

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.930
for individual resources by
navigating to the Resources

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:16.515
tab.

00:04:16.515 --> 00:04:18.390
Click the dropdown list
in the Service column

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:21.670
to select service
enablement on a resource.

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:24.440
Now Security Command Center
is ready to help you prevent,

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:26.320
detect, and respond to threats.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:29.170
This leads up to step number
three, view your dashboards.

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:31.630
Security Command Center gives
you a lot of flexibility

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:34.970
to review the security
state of your resources.

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:37.820
Explore contains a directory
of the dashboards, displays,

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:39.830
and services available to you.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:41.270
On the Threats
dashboard, you can

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:43.700
review potentially harmful
events in your organization's

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:44.750
resources.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:47.030
These events are summarized
by severity, category,

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:48.303
and resource or project.

00:04:48.303 --> 00:04:50.720
The Threats dashboard displays
results for the time period

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.517
you specify in
the dropdown list.

00:04:52.517 --> 00:04:54.350
Click through these
items to see more detail

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:55.880
on the specific findings.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.100
The Vulnerabilities dashboard
display Security Health

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:00.073
Analytics findings
and recommendations.

00:05:00.073 --> 00:05:02.240
The findings can be filtered
to one or more projects

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:03.870
and also by other
characteristics,

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:06.782
such as categories,
severity, and/or benchmarks.

00:05:06.782 --> 00:05:08.240
The Recommendations
column provides

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:10.490
a summary of how to
remediate the finding.

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:13.130
More details on the finding can
be viewed by clicking through.

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:15.710
The Compliance dashboard uses
Security Health Analytics

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:17.510
and Web Security
Scanner detectors

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:19.940
to monitor for violations
against common compliance

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:22.340
controls, which are part of
various industry regimes,

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:25.932
including CIS and PCI.

00:05:25.932 --> 00:05:27.890
While not a replacement
for a compliance audit,

00:05:27.890 --> 00:05:29.390
it can be used to
help you maintain

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:32.910
your continuous compliance
and catch violations early.

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:34.560
You can filter by
project and view

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.430
or export reports of specific
findings by compliance regime.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:41.430
If you'd like to export Security
Command Center data, including

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:43.680
assets and findings, you
can find the Export button

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:48.828
on the Assets or Finding
tabs on the dashboard.

00:05:48.828 --> 00:05:51.370
You can also have event threat
detection and container threat

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:54.340
detection findings automatically
logged to Cloud Logging.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:56.260
To enable, navigate
to Settings and click

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:59.030
on the Sinks tab, where you
can turn on the service.

00:05:59.030 --> 00:05:59.760
Congratulations.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:00.310
That's it.

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:02.740
You're set up to start using
Security Command Center.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:06.390
[MUSIC PLAYING]

